# Recording WebGL (Three.js) Demo Videos with Playwright on Apple Silicon

## 1. Does Playwright `recordVideo` capture WebGL in headless Chromium (2026)?

Yes — but only when the WebGL canvas is actually producing composited pixels. Playwright's `recordVideo` is not a separate capture path; on Chromium it drives the CDP method `Page.startScreencast`, which captures the **composited page** (including the WebGL canvas) as JPEG frames, then pipes them to a bundled `ffmpeg` encoding VP8/webm at a fixed 25 fps.

- `crPage.ts` sends `Page.startScreencast` with `{ width, height, quality }`: https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/3827650d171cc1b035cbefb7e00bf5948d6809df/packages/playwright-core/src/server/chromium/crPage.ts
- `videoRecorder.ts` runs `ffmpeg ... -c:v vp8 -qmin 0 -qmax 50 -crf 8 -deadline realtime -speed 8 -b:v 1M -r 25` and notes: "it is important to ... send Screencast.startScreencast": https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/3827650d171cc1b035cbefb7e00bf5948d6809df/packages/playwright-core/src/server/videoRecorder.ts
- Docs: videos saved on context close, `page.video().path()` only valid after close: https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/3827650d171cc1b035cbefb7e00bf5948d6809df/docs/src/videos.md

**Known caveats — black canvas.** The recording faithfully captures whatever is composited, so a black WebGL canvas means WebGL itself fell back to a non-rendering path. Two root causes dominate on macOS headless:

- **SwiftShader blocked by default.** Chrome blocks the SwiftShader software WebGL backend unless you pass `--enable-unsafe-swiftshader`. This is corroborated by many WebGL test suites (maputnik, vtk-js, geojs, Hilo3d, Sparticuz/chromium) and the `chromedp/headless-shell` README which launches with `--disable-gpu --enable-unsafe-swiftshader`: https://github.com/chromedp/docker-headless-shell/blob/main/README.md . *(I could not retrieve the canonical Chromium monorail issue via the read-only tools; the flag requirement is inferred from convergent upstream configs, not a single primary bug.)*
- **`chrome-headless-shell` vs new headless.** Since Chrome 112 headless is unified ("new headless" creates hidden platform windows, GPU works like headful): https://developer.chrome.com/docs/chromium/headless . Since Chrome 132 the old implementation ships only as the standalone `chrome-headless-shell` binary: https://developer.chrome.com/blog/chrome-headless-shell . Playwright ≥1.57 uses Chrome for Testing (`chrome-headless-shell` for headless, `chrome` for headed); the `chromium` channel and `chrome`/`msedge` channels use new headless (Playwright 1.49 release notes). The headless-shell binary has weaker GPU support than new headless — prefer `channel: 'chrome'` or headed for GPU WebGL.

Playwright 1.59 added `page.screencast.start({ path })` / `stop()` with precise control and an `onFrame` JPEG stream — same CDP mechanism, better API for demos: https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/3827650d171cc1b035cbefb7e00bf5948d6809df/docs/src/release-notes-js.md

## 2. Chrome flags for GPU-accelerated WebGL on Apple Silicon headless

On macOS ARM, ANGLE over Metal is the native GPU path (`--use-angle=metal`): https://github.com/google/angle/blob/main/doc/DebuggingTips.md , and e.g. mapbox-gl-js selects it per-platform. A reliable GPU headless launch:

```js
const browser = await chromium.launch({
  headless: true,
  args: [
    '--use-gl=angle',
    '--use-angle=metal',
    '--enable-gpu-rasterization',
    '--ignore-gpu-blocklist',
    '--enable-unsafe-swiftshader', // fallback if GPU init fails
  ],
});
```

Do **not** pass `--disable-gpu`. Verify with `chrome://gpu` (`--allow-chrome-scheme-url --print-to-pdf chrome://gpu`). If GPU still fails, fall back to software: `--use-angle=swiftshader --enable-unsafe-swiftshader` (slower but deterministic, no black canvas).

## 3. Alternatives & recommendation

**(a) In-page `MediaRecorder` on `canvas.captureStream(60)` → webm blob pulled out via Playwright.** `captureStream()` is Baseline-widely-available and returns a `CanvasCaptureMediaStreamTrack` captured directly from the canvas backing store (bypasses compositing entirely — no black-canvas risk): https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/HTMLCanvasElement/captureStream . Highest fidelity and framerate control; tradeoff is VP8/VP9-only webm (Chrome's `MediaRecorder`), needs post-encode to mp4.

**(b) Per-frame `page.screenshot()` sequence → ffmpeg.** Deterministic and trivially frame-locked, but slow (each screenshot is a round-trip) and misses inter-frame motion unless you drive the sim per-frame.

**(c) ffmpeg `avfoundation` screen capture of a headed window.** Most authentic (real GPU, real window), but non-deterministic timing and requires a logged-in macOS session — poor for CI.

**(d) CDP `Page.startScreencast` directly.** Exactly what Playwright already uses; only worth hand-rolling if you need raw JPEG control: https://chromedevtools.github.io/devtools-protocol/tot/Page/#method-startScreencast

**Recommendation: primary = (a) in-page MediaRecorder; fallback = Playwright `page.screencast.start`.**

Primary (MediaRecorder) — inject via `addInitScript`, pull the blob out:

```js
await context.addInitScript(() => {
  window.__rec = async () => {
    const c = document.querySelector('canvas');
    const stream = c.captureStream(60);
    const mr = new MediaRecorder(stream, { mimeType: 'video/webm;codecs=vp9' });
    const chunks = [];
    mr.ondataavailable = e => e.data.size && chunks.push(e.data);
    window.__stop = () => new Promise(res => {
      mr.onstop = () => res(URL.createObjectURL(new Blob(chunks, {type:'video/webm'})));
      mr.stop();
    });
    mr.start();
  };
});
await page.goto(URL); await page.evaluate(() => window.__rec());
// ... drive gameplay ...
const blobUrl = await page.evaluate(() => window.__stop());
const buf = await page.evaluate(u => fetch(u).then(r=>r.arrayBuffer()), blobUrl);
require('fs').writeFileSync('demo.webm', Buffer.from(buf));
```

Fallback (Playwright screencast):

```js
await page.screencast.start({ path: 'demo.webm', size: { width: 1280, height: 720 } });
// ... drive gameplay ...
await page.screencast.stop();
```

## 4. Deterministic scripted input

`page.keyboard.down('ArrowUp')` / `up()` emit real key events; hold a key by leaving it down between steps. Gate each input on a sim signal (e.g. `await page.waitForFunction(() => window.__frame === N)`) rather than `setTimeout`. For determinism: drive Three.js with a **fixed timestep** and a **seeded RNG** (e.g. `Math.seedrandom`/`mulberry32` injected via `addInitScript`), disable `requestAnimationFrame`'s wall-clock coupling by stepping the sim manually per recorded frame, and use `page.clock` (Playwright Clock API) to freeze time. Verified keyboard API in release notes (v1.45 `ControlOrMeta`, v1.50 traces show pressed keys): https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/3827650d171cc1b035cbefb7e00bf5948d6809df/docs/src/release-notes-js.md

## 5. ffmpeg → clean mp4

Webm → mp4 (re-encode for clean yuv420p):

```
ffmpeg -y -i demo.webm -c:v libx264 -pix_fmt yuv420p -movflags +faststart -crf 18 -preset slow demo.mp4
```

PNG sequence → mp4 (deterministic frame rate):

```
ffmpeg -y -framerate 60 -i frame_%06d.png -c:v libx264 -pix_fmt yuv420p -movflags +faststart -crf 18 -preset slow demo.mp4
```

`yuv420p` ensures broad player compatibility; `+faststart` moves the moov atom for web streaming. VP8 tuning reference: https://trac.ffmpeg.org/wiki/Encode/VP8 (cited inside Playwright's own recorder comments).

---

**Remaining uncertainty:** I could not retrieve the specific Chromium monorail issue that made SwiftShader opt-in (`--enable-unsafe-swiftshader`); the claim rests on convergent upstream configs rather than a primary bug ticket. The Metal/GPU headless flag set is well-corroborated but I did not execute it on Apple Silicon hardware — verify with `chrome://gpu`. No other follow-up needed.
